<h3>Aronde P60 (1961)</h3>
<p>Published in Auto Plus Classiques HS027, the test of the Aronde P60 highlights a significant evolution compared to the original Aronde thanks to a modernized style.
<h4>Technical characteristics </h4>
<br> - Engine: 4-cylinder in-line
<br> - Displacement: 1,290 cm³
<br> - Power: approximately 48 hp
<br> - Gearbox: 4 speeds
<br> - Maximum speed: approximately 135 km/h
<br> - Consumption: approximately 8 L/100 km
<br> - Available in several versions: sedan, coupé, and convertible.
<h4>History and characteristics of the Simca Aronde P60</h4>
<h5># 1. Presentation and evolution</h5>
<br> - The Aronde P60 was launched in 1958 under the name "P60" for "Personalization 1960s."
<br> - It is distinguished by a modernized body with a redesigned grille and a more spacious cabin.
<br> - Available in several finishes and engine options, it was a great success upon its launch, with more than 194,000 units sold in 1959.
<h5># 2. Engine</h5>
<br> - The P60 initially retained the old Aronde engines before introducing the Étoile 6 version in 1960, equipped with the Rush engine.
<br> - This 1,090 cm³ 4-cylinder engine brought better robustness and smoother operation.
<h5># 3. Performance and driving</h5>
<br> - The P60 Étoile 6 developed 42 hp SAE, allowing it to reach approximately 90 km/h on the road.
<br> - Its rigid rear suspension could cause some discomfort, especially if the tires were overinflated.
<br> - Its gearbox was not synchronized on first gear, requiring a specific driving style.
<h5># 4. Price and production</h5>
<br> - In 1961, annual production reached approximately 164,297 units, marking the beginning of the P60\'s decline, as it faced competition from more modern models.</p>

<div id="bloccenter"><img src="/assets/images/autoretro/simca/aronde/simca_aronde_P60_coupe_plein_ciel.jpg" title="Simca Aronde P60 Coupé Plein Ciel" alt="Simca Aronde P60 Coupé Plein Ciel"></div>
<h2>Conclusion</h2>
<p><br>The Simca Aronde P60 represents a notable but brief evolution of the original Aronde, with a more modern and trendy design. Although it was initially successful, its appeal quickly faded due to changing tastes. Despite this, it remains an emblematic model in the history of the automobile of the 1960s.
<br>Paris Motor Show 1961: In October 1961, at the motor show, Simca unveiled its models for 1962. The Aronde and Ariane models were presented jointly, but the star was the Simca 1000, the brand\'s new small car, intended to compete with the Renault 8.
<br>Simca in 1962: With the arrival of the Simca 1000, the production of the Aronde gradually decreased until January 1964. In 1962, Simca, which had previously held the second place among French manufacturers, fell to fourth place. Annual production amounted to 220,200 cars, about 1,800 fewer than Peugeot. It was also the year when Jean Daninos, head of the Facel bodywork company, ceased producing the Océane and Plein Ciel models, which were sports vehicles.
<br>Simca in 1963: Production of the Aronde ended on May 22, on the assembly lines of the Poissy factory. Production reached 273,617 units, with a large share of exports (126,120 vehicles) to countries such as Italy, Germany, Belgium, and the Netherlands. However, after the elimination of the Étoile 7 and Monaco Spéciale models, only the Étoile 6 and Super Étoile 6 versions remained in production, with a sharp drop in sales in 1964, where only 21 units were produced. In total, 1,425,329 units of the Aronde were produced since 1951.
<br>End of the Aronde: The Aronde, which marked French automotive history for more than two decades, reached the end of its career. It was replaced by the Simca 1300.
<br>In March 1963, at the Geneva Motor Show, Simca presented the Simca 1300 (7 cv), designed to succeed the Aronde. This model, with a modern but classic design, was available in sedan and wagon versions. A Simca 1500 (8 cv) version with a more powerful engine and a modified grille was also offered.
<br>Management transition at Simca: The end of the Aronde coincided with the departure of its creator, H.-T. Pigozzi, who handed over the management of Simca to Georges Héreil, former head of Sud-Aviation. This transition marked a turning point for the brand, which continued to develop under a new direction.
<br>In summary, the P60 is considered a successful but short-lived evolution of the original Aronde, benefiting from a more contemporary but ultimately less timeless style than its predecessor.',

    

